From: FlipperEmu
Subject: [PATCH] armv7m_nvic: pend an asynchronous fault

The NVIC treats every fault as synchronous: a BusFault that is disabled,
or not of a higher priority than the execution, is escalated to
HardFault at once, and where HardFault cannot be taken either that is a
Lockup, which QEMU reports with cpu_abort(). QEMU's own data aborts are
all precise, so this was enough. A board's memory may refuse a write the
way a bus does, though, after the bus has taken it: an imprecise
BusFault, which the architecture makes an asynchronous exception
(ARMv7-M ARM B1.5.8): it is not escalated for its priority, it remains
pending until it can be taken, and a disabled one escalates to a
HardFault that is pending in turn. None of that is a Lockup.

Add armv7m_nvic_set_pending_async() for such a fault.

Seen with the Flipper Zero machine and the STM32WB55's SRAM2, whose
upper parts the wireless stack keeps for CPU2: a write there by CPU1 is
an imprecise BusFault on the chip. A firmware whose shared block reaches
into that part wipes it in its reset handler (BusFault disabled: a
HardFault), and its HardFault handler writes its crash registers there
too, then resets the chip. On the chip that is a loop of resets. With a
synchronous fault QEMU ended at the handler's first write, "Lockup:
can't escalate 3 to HardFault (current priority -1)".

Signed-off-by: FlipperEmu
---
--- a/include/hw/intc/armv7m_nvic.h
+++ b/include/hw/intc/armv7m_nvic.h
@@ -123,6 +123,21 @@
  */
 void armv7m_nvic_set_pending_lazyfp(NVICState *s, int irq, bool secure);
 /**
+ * armv7m_nvic_set_pending_async: mark an asynchronous fault as pending
+ * @s: the NVIC
+ * @irq: the exception number to mark pending (BusFault, for an imprecise
+ * one, which a board's memory may raise for a write it does not take)
+ * @secure: false for non-banked exceptions or for the nonsecure
+ * version of a banked exception, true for the secure version of a banked
+ * exception.
+ *
+ * Similar to armv7m_nvic_set_pending(), but for a fault that is not
+ * synchronous: it is not escalated for its priority, and remains pending
+ * until it can be taken.  A disabled one still escalates to HardFault,
+ * which is pending in turn, and is never a Lockup.
+ */
+void armv7m_nvic_set_pending_async(NVICState *s, int irq, bool secure);
+/**
  * armv7m_nvic_get_pending_irq_info: return highest priority pending
  *    exception, and whether it targets Secure state
  * @s: the NVIC
--- a/hw/intc/armv7m_nvic.c
+++ b/hw/intc/armv7m_nvic.c
@@ -694,6 +694,48 @@
     do_armv7m_nvic_set_pending(s, irq, secure, true);
 }
 
+void armv7m_nvic_set_pending_async(NVICState *s, int irq, bool secure)
+{
+    /*
+     * An asynchronous fault (an imprecise BusFault: a write that the bus
+     * took and that failed later) is not escalated for its priority, as
+     * a synchronous one is: it remains pending until it can be taken.  A
+     * disabled one escalates to HardFault, which is then pending; while
+     * the processor runs at HardFault's priority or above, that waits too,
+     * and it is never a Lockup (ARMv7-M ARM B1.5.8, B1.5.15).
+     */
+    bool banked = exc_is_banked(irq);
+    VecInfo *vec;
+    bool targets_secure;
+
+    assert(irq >= ARMV7M_EXCP_HARD && irq < ARMV7M_EXCP_PENDSV);
+    assert(!secure || banked);
+
+    vec = (banked && secure) ? &s->sec_vectors[irq] : &s->vectors[irq];
+    targets_secure = banked ? secure : exc_targets_secure(s, irq);
+
+    trace_nvic_set_pending(irq, secure, targets_secure, false, vec->enabled,
+                           vec->prio);
+
+    if (!vec->enabled) {
+        trace_nvic_escalate_disabled(irq);
+        irq = ARMV7M_EXCP_HARD;
+        if (arm_feature(&s->cpu->env, ARM_FEATURE_M_SECURITY) &&
+            (targets_secure ||
+             !(s->cpu->env.v7m.aircr & R_V7M_AIRCR_BFHFNMINS_MASK))) {
+            vec = &s->sec_vectors[irq];
+        } else {
+            vec = &s->vectors[irq];
+        }
+        s->cpu->env.v7m.hfsr |= R_V7M_HFSR_FORCED_MASK;
+    }
+
+    if (!vec->pending) {
+        nvic_update_pending_state(s, vec, irq, 1);
+        nvic_irq_update(s);
+    }
+}
+
 void armv7m_nvic_set_pending_lazyfp(NVICState *s, int irq, bool secure)
 {
     /*
